From 966a567fd5a9b34017822b4f97567686aff3c130 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Adam Asay <aasay@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 01:19:39 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] UTS and IPC modes ns:<path>: join existing UTS and IPC
 namespaces

Accept Podman's --uts ns:<absolute path> and --ipc ns:<absolute path>
(HostConfig.UTSMode and IpcMode), so containers of a pod made outside
Kiri share its hostname and System V IPC and POSIX message queues as
well as its network (--network ns:<path>). The namespaces are joined as
they are and nothing in them is made or removed.

In a joined UTS namespace the hostname is the namespace's: the child
sets no hostname or domain name, and $HOSTNAME, /etc/hostname and
/etc/hosts name the namespace's hostname, which the agent reads by
stepping into it, as it does for sysctls. Hostname and Domainname from
the client are ignored rather than refused (Podman refuses them), so
Docker clients that always send them keep working.

The daemon validates the path is absolute and keeps the mode, shown by
inspect as UTSMode and IpcMode; it passes the paths to the agent as the
new utsns_path and ipcns_path options. The agent opens each, checks its
type with NS_GET_NSTYPE (open_netns_path becomes open_ns_path for all
three) and joins it in the child instead of cloning a new one, before
the mounts so /dev/mqueue belongs to the joined IPC namespace. Other
UTS and IPC modes are left as before. docker exec works as usual.
